1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,839
Congratulations. You now have a good hand on how to build your own agents.

2
00:00:04,639 --> 00:00:09,120
With what you have learned today, you can build simple or quite complex agents.

3
00:00:09,679 --> 00:00:14,800
But before we go, I wanted to cover some of the agent flows that we couldn't build today,

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:16,399
but I think you should know about.

5
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:23,519
The first architecture I want to talk about is a multi-agent architecture.

6
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:28,560
And we covered this a little bit with the writing agent, but just to make it a little bit more

7
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:35,679
concrete. A multi-agent architecture is when multiple different agents work on the same shared

8
00:00:35,679 --> 00:00:41,920
state. And so these agents could be just a prompt and a language model as they were in the writer.

9
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:46,079
They could also have different tools that they could call this, so they could be a prompt,

10
00:00:46,079 --> 00:00:50,560
a language model, and tools, and they could actually have their own loops inside them.

11
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:55,680
The crucial thing here and the differentiator between the next thing that we'll see is that they

12
00:00:55,679 --> 00:01:02,000
all work on the same shared state, so they're passing that around from one agent to the next.

13
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:07,840
Let's contrast that with a supervisor agent. So here we have a supervisor

14
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:13,200
which is calling into some subagents. And here the supervisor will actually determine what the

15
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:18,640
input to that agent is. And those agents themselves could have different states inside them.

16
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:24,480
They're a graph. And so there's not necessarily the same concept of a shared state.

17
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:30,000
Other than that, this is pretty similar to the multi-agent framework. It just really emphasizes

18
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,359
that there's just one supervisor that's in charge of routing and coordinating these other agents.

19
00:01:36,159 --> 00:01:41,040
This is often good when you can use a really powerful language model as the supervisor,

20
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:45,439
because doing the supervision, doing this planning requires a lot of intelligence.

21
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:51,840
One term that's popped up a bunch recently is flow engineering. This comes from an alpha-coding

22
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:58,400
paper where they achieve state-of-the-art coding performance, and they do it with a very

23
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:05,840
graphical-like solution like this. And so looking at it, you can see that it's basically a pipeline,

24
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:11,840
but there are a few key nodes where there's actually a loop. So there's loops on the initial code

25
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:17,199
solution. There's loops when you iterate on public tests, and there's loops when you iterate on AI

26
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:23,520
tests. And so this is an interesting graph because you have this very directed flow up until a

27
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:31,040
certain point, and then you have these iterations. This is a really bespoke architecture

28
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:37,520
for a particular coding problem, but the concept of flow engineering extends more broadly,

29
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:43,120
and it generally refers to thinking of what is the right information flow for your agents to take

30
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:52,480
action and think. In that vein, a common paradigm is to have a plan and execute style flow,

31
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:59,360
where first you do an explicit planning step up front, and then you start to execute on that plan.

32
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:05,840
So you may come up with a few steps that a sub agent should go do. It can then go do one,

33
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:09,520
come back, maybe you update the plan, maybe you don't, this is where there are some different

34
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:14,880
variants, but then it goes on to the next thing, and it does that, and it comes back and tell us

35
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:20,320
finishes the plan, then you maybe check to see if the plan is successfully accomplished or whether

36
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:26,160
you need to replan, and then if all's good, you return to the user. The final one I want to touch

37
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:32,960
on is a really interesting paper called language agent tree search. So this basically does a tree

38
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:38,320
search over the state of possible actions, and so it first generates an action, it then reflects,

39
00:03:38,879 --> 00:03:44,000
and then goes down based on that action and generates some other sub actions, reflects on that,

40
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,239
and throughout all these reflections, it can basically reflect on where in the tree of action states

41
00:03:50,239 --> 00:03:56,479
it wants to jump back to. So we can back propagate and update parent nodes to have more information

42
00:03:56,479 --> 00:04:03,359
which may inform future directions from that previous state. For this in particular, you can really see

43
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:09,760
by persistence is really important because you need to be able to go back in time to previous states.

44
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:15,280
These are just some of the emerging paradigms for creating more complex,

45
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:21,840
agentic flows. Langgraph is really aimed at being highly controllable and allowing you to create

46
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:29,199
these cyclical or non-cyclical flows. This degree of controllability is really what differentiates it

47
00:04:29,199 --> 00:04:33,839
from other frameworks, and it's what we're really excited about going forward,

48
00:04:33,839 --> 00:04:44,079
as we view this to be crucial to creating agents that actually work.
